      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Laparoscopic Roux-en-Y gastric bypass (RYGB) induces a more favorable metabolic profile than expected by weight loss alone. In this study, we investigated the effect of RYGB on serum bile acid levels and their relation to clinical outcomes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We included 30 obese patients who underwent RYGB (BMI\u2009=\u200946.1\u2009±\u20095.9 kg/m(2)). Clinical measurements and laboratory determinations were performed before surgery and 1 year after surgery. Fasting serum bile acids were measured by an enzymatic method and individual bile acids were quantified by HLPC-tandem mass spectrometry. Indirect calorimetry was performed to measure the rates of energy expenditure and substrate oxidation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Fasting total serum bile acid levels increased twofold after RYGB (pre, 3.68\u2009±\u20092.03 vs. post, 7.06\u2009±\u20099.65 μmol/l, +92 %, p\u2009=\u20090.002). This increase in total bile acids was accompanied by a decrease in conjugated bile acids, which correlated with decreased glucose oxidation (r\u2009=\u20090.571, p\u2009=\u20090.002) and with increased lipid oxidation (r\u2009=\u2009-0.626, p\u2009=\u20090.0004). The change in taurine-conjugated bile acids correlated with altered DIO2 mRNA expression in adipose tissue (r\u2009=\u2009-0.498, p\u2009=\u20090.013) potentially linking bile acid conjugation to substrate oxidation through DIO2."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Fasting serum bile acid levels increase after RYGB. More specifically, changes in bile acid conjugation after RYGB associate with altered energy metabolism."
        }
      ]},
